| Opus 5.5 costs $4/$20, Sonnet 5.5 costs $2/$10 per million tokens | Anthropic pricing page | Verified, first-party |
| Sonnet 5.5 cache reads cost $0.10 after an October 7 cut | Anthropic post and pricing page | Verified, first-party |
| Both models accept five effort levels, low to max | Anthropic effort docs | Verified, first-party |
| Opus 5.5 defaults to medium, Sonnet 5.5 to high on the API | Anthropic effort docs | Verified, first-party |
| Subagent `model` and `effort` frontmatter, with documented precedence | Claude Code sub-agents docs | Verified, first-party |
| Skill `model` and `effort` frontmatter exist, and apply for the current turn | Claude Code skills docs | Verified, first-party; not tested |
| `opusplan` uses Opus in plan mode, then Sonnet | Claude Code model configuration docs | Verified, first-party |
| Sonnet at max scored below xhigh on FrontierCode because of review-skill fan-out | Footnote on Anthropic's Sonnet 5.5 page | Vendor-reported, partner examined |
| Opus 5.5 leads on FrontierCode, 54.4 vs 52.1 (xhigh) vs 46.2 (max) | Anthropic's Sonnet 5.5 page | Vendor-reported |
| Opus 5.5 42 vs 46 percent on a merge-graded coding benchmark at max | Artificial Analysis Index v5 | Independent |
| Skill invocation loads the body once and keeps it in context | Claude Code skills docs | Verified, first-party |
| Sonnet is better than Opus at following skills | None | Not established; test it |
| A skill file's size dominates session cost | Arithmetic above | Contradicted for typical sizes; effort and fan-out dominate |

## Running Your Own Routing Experiment

The cost arithmetic above is illustrative, so replace it with your own. Pick three skills you actually use, one planning skill, one execution skill and one review skill. For each, define a fixed set of ten tasks with known good outcomes, a mix of easy and hard. Run each task under four configurations: Sonnet 5.5 at medium, Sonnet 5.5 at high, Opus 5.5 at medium, and Opus 5.5 at high. Record whether the result passed your checks, whether a reviewer would merge it without edits, the number of subagents launched, total output tokens, and wall-clock time. Then compute cost per accepted change using the formula in this article, which is the number that matters, instead of cost per attempt.

Two rules keep the experiment honest. Run every configuration on a clean branch so no model sees a previous attempt. And compare the number of subagents launched, because the Cognition footnote suggests more effort can change how a skill behaves, not just how long the model thinks. If effort at high launches twice as many reviewers as medium and the merge rate does not improve, you found a cap to add to the skill.

Add xhigh and max only for the hard tasks and only where high showed a gain. Anthropic's own guidance says to use those levels only where your evals show a quality gain. Once you have a table of cost per accepted change by skill, encode it: set `model` and `effort` in the skill's frontmatter or on the subagents each skill delegates to, use `opusplan` for interactive planning, and leave the session default at Sonnet 5.5 medium. For broader context on model-driven agent workloads and a worked cost analysis of another frontier pair, read the [GPT-6.1 Sol agent workloads breakdown](/blog/gpt-6-1-sol-agent-workloads), and for a scorecard-style approach to a new model release, see the [Muse Wonder scorecard for agent builders](/blog/muse-wonder-meta-scorecard-agent-builders).

{% youtube id="1f13Bl1sYkw" title="Introducing Claude Opus 5.5" /%}

{% youtube id="s5nkj-L2vAw" title="Introducing Claude Sonnet 5.5" /%}

Both videos are Anthropic's official product introductions on the Claude YouTube channel. This guide takes its numbers from the written pages and documentation, which carry the footnotes the videos omit.

{% x id="2107894060229034197" /%}

## Limitations

This guide inherits the limits of its sources. Every Anthropic benchmark is vendor-reported, and several, including AutomationBench, are partner benchmarks. The two models' rows in Anthropic's tables are not always at the same effort level, and the Terminal-Bench 4.0 row does not state the effort Sonnet ran at. Differences of a few points sit inside the stated standard errors.

The skill-specific advice is partly inference. Anthropic has not published a benchmark of skill compliance for either model, so the routing table's reasoning rests on documented behavior, price arithmetic and the FrontierCode footnote, and nothing here was measured on a skill workload. I did not run any of these skills against either model. The four-characters-per-token estimate is a rule of thumb, not a tokenizer count. The Claude Code default effort for Sonnet 5.5 is stated differently on two Anthropic pages, so check `/effort` in your own session. Claude Code ships changes quickly, so check the current skills and sub-agents pages before relying on any frontmatter field. I did not test skill-level `model` or `effort` frontmatter myself; I am reporting what the docs say.

## Verdict

Use Opus 5.5 for the steps that decide what to build and whether it is good, and Sonnet 5.5 for the steps that do the building many times. Set effort on purpose: medium for specified work, high for hard or open-ended work, and treat xhigh and max as experiments with a measured payoff. Cap subagent fan-out in review skills, because that is where Anthropic's own footnote says more effort stopped helping. Do not trim `SKILL.md` files for cost reasons alone; trim them so the important rules sit at the top and survive compaction.

The honest unknown is skill compliance. Until someone publishes a skill-following benchmark for these models, the routing table is a hypothesis built from price, effort guidance and one revealing footnote. Run the ten-task experiment above on your three most-used skills before you change a team default.

## FAQ

**Should a skill run on Opus 5.5 or Sonnet 5.5?**
Run execution-style skills on Sonnet 5.5 at medium effort and planning or final-review steps on Opus 5.5. Claude Code's `opusplan` mode applies that split automatically for plan mode versus execution. Test your own skills before treating this as a rule, because Anthropic has not published a skill-compliance benchmark for either model.

**How do I set the model and effort for a subagent?**
In the subagent's frontmatter, set `model` to an alias, a full model ID or `inherit`, and set `effort` to low, medium, high, xhigh or max. The `effort` field overrides the session level but not the `CLAUDE_CODE_EFFORT_LEVEL` environment variable. The model order is the per-invocation parameter, the frontmatter field, `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL`, then the main conversation's model.

**What are the effort levels called, and what is the default?**
The API effort parameter accepts low, medium, high, xhigh and max on both Opus 5.5 and Sonnet 5.5. Opus 5.5 defaults to medium and Sonnet 5.5 defaults to high on the API. Anthropic's Sonnet page says the default in Claude Code and its apps is Medium, which differs from the docs wording for the previous Sonnet, so confirm with `/effort`.

**Does a bigger SKILL.md make Claude Code more expensive?**
Slightly. An invoked skill body stays in context and is billed as a cache read on later turns. For a 4,000-token body over a 30-turn session, the arithmetic above gives about $0.043 on Opus 5.5 and $0.022 on Sonnet 5.5. A single 30,000-token high-effort output turn costs $0.60 on Opus, so effort and subagent count matter more than file size.

**How much cheaper is Sonnet 5.5 than Opus 5.5 for the same skill run?**
Every Sonnet price is half the Opus price, so identical token counts cost half as much. If Sonnet uses twice the tokens, the cost is equal. In the six-subagent example above, Sonnet costs $1.68 against $3.36 for Opus, and a mixed Opus-plans, Sonnet-executes routing costs $1.86. Token counts there are illustrative.

**Why did Sonnet 5.5 score lower at max effort than at xhigh on FrontierCode?**
Anthropic's footnote says that at max, Sonnet 5.5 more often ran Claude Code's code-review skill, which splits a review across many subagents. In two cases Cognition examined, that caused a timeout or edits beyond the task's scope, which FrontierCode penalizes. The takeaway is that more effort can mean more activity, not necessarily a smaller mergeable change.

**Can I switch from Sonnet 5.5 to Opus 5.5 mid-session without losing reasoning?**
Going from Sonnet 5.5 to Opus 5.5 keeps the reasoning trail on the Claude API, because Opus 5.5 reads Sonnet 5.5's thinking blocks. Going the other way, Sonnet 5.5 does not read Opus 5.5's thinking blocks, so later turns run without it. The request still succeeds and the dropped blocks are not billed.

